
`native/` holds the AUnit tests for ImplementPlough/InterfacePlough and the
serial-path GPS sentence parsers (../lib/PloegbesturingCore/src/), run
natively (no Teensy attached):

- `native/tests/` -- one `test_<ClassName>.cpp` per class under test, or a
  descriptive group name for a related set (`test_GpsParsers.cpp` covers
  `NmeaParser`/`TrimbleParser`/`CanSerialParser`).
- `native/support/` -- a small, self-contained set of Arduino-shaped stubs
  (Print/Stream/EEPROM/Arduino.h/etc.), not a real EpoxyDuino dependency, plus
  `native_main.cpp` (the host `int main()` entry point). `GuidanceSource.hpp`
  here is a fake with the same API shape as the real class in
  `lib/PloegbesturingCore/src/guidance/` -- both `ImplementPlough`/
  `InterfacePlough` tests (which poke raw fields directly) and
  `test_GpsParsers.cpp` (which calls real `Set*()`/`Get*()` methods) resolve
  to this same fake, since `[env:native]`'s `-I test/native/support` is
  searched before the real header's directory.
- `native/PloegbesturingNativeTests.cpp` -- the single AUnit driver (`setup()`/
  `loop()`), matching Salacia's/Loofdoes' `SalaciaNativeTests.cpp`/
  `LoofdoesNativeTests.cpp` pattern. Every `test_*.cpp` file only registers
  `test(...)` blocks; none of them define `setup()`/`loop()` themselves, so
  they all link into one combined binary instead of one binary per test file.
- `native/run_tests_msvc.ps1` -- MSVC fallback for Windows without GCC.

```
pio run -e native
.pio/build/native/program(.exe)
```

Needs a real host C++17 compiler on PATH (g++ or clang++) -- on Windows
without one, use test\native\run_tests_msvc.ps1 instead, which builds and
runs the same combined binary via MSVC directly. That script passes
`/Zc:preprocessor` -- MSVC's legacy preprocessor mishandles AUnit's
`test(SuiteName, testName)` argument-count dispatch, which every test in
this project uses (matching Salacia's/Loofdoes' convention: groups each
test's display name under the class it exercises, e.g.
`ImplementPlough_position_descendingCalibration_atPoint0`,
`NmeaParser_gga_lat_lon_quality_alt`).

## Real-board envs

Two buildable board environments, both `board = teensy41`, selected by
whether guidance data comes over CAN/ISOBUS or plain UART:

- `pio run -e teensy41_isobus` (default) -- `IsobusGuidanceChannel`, AgIsoStack
  address claim + PGN subscriptions/requests, plus the Virtual Terminal
  client and diagnostics (`IsobusVtInterface`/`VTObjectPool`). `-D ISOBUS`.
- `pio run -e teensy41_serial` -- `SerialGuidanceChannel`, plain UART
  NMEA/Trimble/CAN-over-serial parsing (`GpsParser`/`NmeaParser`/
  `TrimbleParser`/`CanSerialParser`). No CAN bus, no AgIsoStack, no VT.

Both share `[env:teensy41]` as a common template in `platformio.ini` (not
built directly), mirroring Salacia's own base-env + variant pattern.

## Known gaps

- `CalibrationPlough` (the LCD/button calibration wizard extracted from the
  legacy `InterfacePlough::Calibrate()`) is `#ifdef ARDUINO`-guarded and
  deliberately excluded from `[env:native]`'s `build_src_filter` -- it drives
  real LCD/button hardware through a sequence of blocking `while(true)` menu
  loops, the same shape of problem Loofdoes' `CalibrationSprayer` has, and
  which is excluded there for the same reason. No native coverage exists or
  is planned for it.
- `InterfacePlough::UpdateScreen()` (LCD rendering) compiles as part of
  `InterfacePlough.cpp` (needed since `CheckButtons()`/`Update()`, which
  *are* tested, live in the same file) but its output isn't asserted on by
  any test -- `test/native/support/InterfaceI2CLCD.hpp`'s `WriteBuffer`/
  `WriteScreen` are no-ops.
- `IsobusGuidanceChannel`/`IsobusVtInterface`/`SerialGuidanceChannel` are all
  thin hardware/transport adapters (CAN bus, ISOBUS stack, or raw UART byte
  reading) with no native coverage by design -- the real logic worth testing
  (PGN byte-decode math, sentence parsing) lives in the natively-tested
  classes each of them calls into (`GuidanceSource`, `NmeaParser`/
  `TrimbleParser`/`CanSerialParser`).
- `teensy41_isobus`: the JD/Trimble/CNH legacy proprietary-PGN decode and the
  standard NMEA2000 PGN decode both build and link against the real AgIsoStack
  package, but haven't been verified against real CAN traffic on target
  hardware -- see `IsobusGuidanceChannel.cpp`'s comments for the specific
  open risks (PDU1 destination-addressing for the Trimble/CNH message,
  whether non-address-claiming senders are delivered via the PGN callback at
  all).
- `teensy41_serial`: builds and links, but the UART sentence dispatch itself
  (`SerialGuidanceChannel::Update()`/`dispatchTerm()`, including the Trimble
  binary packet framing) has not been verified against a real GPS receiver.
- Only the `teensy41` board is wired up today. Ploegbesturing has
  historically also run on Arduino Uno, Arduino Micro, Teensy 3.2, and
  Teensy 3.6 -- their pin-config `#ifdef` branches remain in
  `ConfigPlough.hpp`/`ConfigImplementPlough.hpp`/`ConfigInterfacePlough.hpp`
  for future revival, but none of them has a buildable `platformio.ini` env
  yet.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing (not used here -- pio test's
own Unity-based runner doesn't understand this project's AUnit setup):
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html
